## Where it lives

These features sit inside tools the content team already had open. Nobody wants a
separate AI product to go and visit, so the entry points are on the pages where the
work was happening anyway: a suggestion beside the draft, a report on the screen that
used to be assembled by hand, a search box that accepts a sentence instead of a set of
filters.

## Designing for the wrong answer

The interesting part of shipping an LLM feature is not the happy path. It is what the
product does when the model returns something malformed, wrong, or slow. Every call has
a defined output shape and gets validated before anything downstream touches it.
Prompts are versioned, so when quality shifts there is something to compare against.
A person stays in the loop on anything that gets published: suggestions stay
suggestions, and nothing posts itself.

## What stuck

Draft suggestions, automated reporting that replaced a manual assembly step, and
plain-language search over the team's own data. The features that became part of the
daily routine were the ones that removed a step somebody repeated every day. A couple
of ideas that demoed well were dropped, because on closer look they added a decision
rather than removing one.

## Stack

Python against the OpenAI API, built into the team's existing internal tools.
